WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.250
In the late 1930s, a federal agency
graded the neighborhoods of US cities and

2
00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:07.170
colored them on maps.

3
00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:10.990
The agency was the Home
Owners' Loan Corporation.

4
00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:12.870
Green meant Best.

5
00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:14.930
Red meant Hazardous.

6
00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:21.810
In 2018, a study compared these maps
from 115 cities with recent census data.

7
00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:26.530
Many areas once graded red are
still low-to-moderate income today.

8
00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:32.170
How can a map from the late 1930s still
match where people are poorer today?

9
00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:36.530
In this mission, you'll uncover the
practices that reshaped neighborhoods.

10
00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:41.110
Then you'll judge how cities respond,
from affordable-housing rules to community

11
00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:42.410
gardens.

12
00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:47.990
Your mission: find out why maps from the
1930s still match where many US cities

13
00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:49.480
are poorer today.
